## Key Learnings

1. Pekko's ProducerController arms a ResendFirst timer for every first message regardless of a durable queue, contradicting its own Scaladoc and the reconciliation texts of three stories.
2. The first SequencedMessage is sent without any consumer Request, so acceptance criteria that forbid sending before demand contradict ProducerControllerImpl and ProducerControllerSpec.
3. The work-pulling controller sends every user message to its per-worker ProducerController as MessageWithConfirmation, so work pulling depends on the point-to-point ack path.
4. No Pekko spec covers the work-pulling buffer limit, the LoadState retry exhaustion, or any stale-assembly lifetime, and the buffer overflow is unreachable for a protocol-conforming producer in non-durable mode.
5. Pekko's own actor-typed-tests ship an in-memory TestDurableProducerQueue with specs, so claims of no oracle for producer-queue protocol recovery are incorrect.
